---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const modCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Three statements, one engine', note: 'Model the income statement (sales → profit), balance sheet (what funds it), cash flow (what actually arrives) — linked by formulas. Every assumption visible in one cell.' },
    { math: '2 · Drivers, not patches', note: 'Model the BUSINESS logic: units × price, growth rates, margins as functions of scale. Hard-coding outcomes makes the model a slideshow; driver-based models answer questions.' },
    { math: '3 · Scenarios: the point of the exercise', note: 'Base / optimistic / pessimistic cases, switched at one cell. The output is a RANGE of outcomes and which assumptions matter most — sensitivity analysis is the model\'s real product.' },
    { math: '4 · Hygiene is survival', note: 'Separate inputs from calculations; one formula per row; colour-code inputs; check the balance sheet balances. Spreadsheet errors are common and expensive (the EU\'s austerity spreadsheet error is the famous case).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/advanced/financial-modelling-for-managers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A financial model is <strong>the business as an equation</strong>: assumptions in, three
    statements and a valuation out. Its purpose is decision support — testing "what if we cut
    price 10%", "what if growth halves" before the real money moves. The craft is driver-based
    structure plus honest scenario analysis.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="statements, drivers, scenarios, hygiene" widget="equationStep" config={modCfg} />

  <h2>The standard builds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three-statement model:</strong> the foundation — income, balance, cash flow linked, balance sheet checking to zero. Every other model extends it.</li>
    <li><strong>DCF valuation:</strong> free cash flows discounted at WACC (the corporate-finance lesson) → enterprise value. Garbage forecasts in, confident garbage out — sensitivity ranges are mandatory.</li>
    <li><strong>Budget vs actual:</strong> the operating rhythm — variance analysis tells management WHERE the model was wrong, feeding the next iteration. Models are conversations with reality.</li>
    <li><strong>Unit-economics models:</strong> CAC, LTV, contribution margin per customer — the startup's model is smaller and more honest than the corporate one, and answers the survival question directly (the unit-economics lesson).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rosenbaum & Pearl, Investment Banking; Wall Street Prep / CFI curricula; the EU "empty spreadsheet cell" case (2015).</p>
</TopicPage>
